# {{ title }}

**Abstract:** This technical report details the rigorous 1D steady-state physical modeling and machine-learning surrogate optimization of a tube-in-tube Catalytic Membrane Reactor (CMR) for E-Methanol synthesis (Module A, NaA zeolite water-selective membrane) and Ammonia Decomposition (Module B, metallic Pd hydrogen-selective membrane). By selectively removing inhibitory reaction byproducts ($H_2O$ or $H_2$) directly from the catalytic zone, the reactor circumvents thermodynamic equilibrium limits dictated by Le Chatelier's principle. Under optimized operating conditions ($T = 250^\circ\text{C}, P = 100\text{ bar}, \text{GHSV} = 500\text{ h}^{-1}, O_M/V_r = 133.33\text{ m}^{-1}, S/F = 10$), the membrane reactor achieves **{{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['X_MR']) }}%** single-pass $CO_2$ conversion compared to **{{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['X_TR']) }}%** in a traditional plug-flow reactor—a decisive gain of **+{{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['Gain_X']) }} percentage points** (+{{ "%0.1f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['Gain_X'] / results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['X_TR'] * 100) }}% relative increase) with **{{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['Water_Removal']) }}%** in-situ water removal. Applying a trans-membrane pressure driving force of 99 bar further elevates conversion to **{{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case + dP 99 bar']['X_MR']) }}%**.

