## 9. Discussion

### 9.1 Resolving the Open Literature Discrepancy
In Hauth et al. (2025), Figure 12 clearly charts the "Best Case" conversion of $45.3\%$ utilizing an annular geometry with $d_w = 20\text{ mm}$, yielding $O_M/V_r = 133.33\text{ m}^{-1}$. However, Section 3.2 text ambiguously mentions $O_M/V_r = 26.67\text{ m}^{-1}$. By independently simulating both geometries, our model definitively explains the discrepancy:
- At $O_M/V_r = 26.67\text{ m}^{-1}$, the reactor achieves only **{{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Text Discrepancy)']['Water_Removal']) }}%** water removal, capping conversion at **{{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Text Discrepancy)']['X_MR']) }}%**.
- At $O_M/V_r = 133.33\text{ m}^{-1}$, water removal surges to **{{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['Water_Removal']) }}%**, matching the reported **{{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case (Fig 12 Discrepancy)']['X_MR']) }}%** curve.

### 9.2 Techno-Economic Trade-Offs
While increasing sweep gas flow ($S/F = 10$) maximizes trans-membrane concentration gradients, it dilutes the permeate effluent with nitrogen, introducing downstream condensation, refrigeration, and re-compression energy costs. In practical installations, operating under a permeate vacuum ($\Delta P = 99\text{ bar}$) without sweep gas achieves nearly identical intensification (**{{ "%0.2f"|format(results_a['Best Case + dP 99 bar']['X_MR']) }}%** conversion) while delivering pure condensable water and concentrated retentate.

